            "Microsoft says attackers have shortened the \"data exfiltration cycle, credential discovery and lateral movement lifecycle from days to minutes\". Its own framing of the significance, quoted by Infosecurity: \"While none of these represent new attack methods, the quantitative increase in the scale and speed of attacks creates an immediate problem for defenders.\"",
            "Most-targeted sectors in Microsoft's telemetry are government at 27% of attacks, IT at 17% and research and academia at 14%; by country the US saw 25.5%, then Israel at 7.6%, Ukraine at 4.8% and Taiwan at 3.9%.",
            "The report's headline finding — that attackers are reaching AI's advantages before defenders — ran in yesterday's briefing; these are the breakdown figures. Everything here is Microsoft's own telemetry and Microsoft's own attribution of the public-facing-application rise to AI tooling, which no third party has verified. Infosecurity also quotes the report saying \"most of what we exploit there has nothing to do with AI\"."
          ],
          "topics": ["threat-intel", "cyber-offense", "microsoft", "cyber-defense"],
          "storylines": ["ai-enabled-hacking"],
