          "headline": "Google moves Gemini to compute-based usage limits from 9 October and restricts Pro to its two paid top tiers",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "Google", "url": "https://support.google.com/gemini/answer/17004136" },
            { "name": "XDA Developers", "url": "https://www.xda-developers.com/google-ai-plus-users-are-losing-access-to-geminis-pro-model-and-its-happening-very-soon/" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "Google's support page says that \"starting in October 2026, there will be changes to model availability for Gemini Apps when you use a personal account\", with changes beginning \"October 9th\" for users without subscriptions. Its table gives Flash-Lite and Flash to accounts without a plan and to AI Plus, and adds Pro only for AI Pro and AI Ultra.",
            "The page also says \"Gemini will move to compute-based usage limits that will refresh every 5 hours\": standard limits without a plan, 2x higher for AI Plus, 4x higher for AI Pro, and \"5x or 20x higher than AI Pro depending on your subscription\" for AI Ultra. A five-hour compute budget is a different unit from a message count, and the page gives no conversion.",
            "The practical effect on the paid middle tier is a downgrade: AI Plus subscribers lose Pro. XDA Developers, reporting at 1:56 AM EDT on 3 October, says Google told Plus subscribers they will receive an email detailing when they lose Pro access.",
            "XDA's headline claim that free users will lose Flash and be left with only Flash-Lite is not what the support page we read says — its table lists Flash-Lite and Flash for accounts without a plan. We report the primary page and flag the discrepancy; one of the two is out of date."
          ],
          "topics": ["google-deepmind", "deployment", "labor"],
          "impact": "mixed",
