      "type": "intro",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "It's Saturday, October 3rd, and this is The AI Edge, presented by Epilogue." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Epilogue is an AI venture studio and consultancy in Toronto, building products where the answer has to be right. Epilogue ships systems that know what they know, show their work, and fail visibly instead of quietly. Visit epiloguelabs.com to learn more." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "I'm Maya." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And I'm Alex." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Here's what happened at the frontier of AI in the last 24 hours: what shipped, what got published, and where the technology is being used, for good and for harm. Every claim in this episode is linked to its source on the site." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "What's leading?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "First, a federal judge in Oklahoma ruled that a warrantless police search of Flock's automated licence plate reader network was unconstitutional, and called that network a type of indiscriminate mass surveillance." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Second, Cerebras fell nearly 20% this week to its lowest price since its May stock market debut, after a report that OpenAI moved its fastest inference mode onto Nvidia hardware. CNBC says the market cap now sits at just over $39 billion." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And third, we finally have per-site figures for the AI agents that have been probing government websites, including over 200,000 requests at one US Department of Education site." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Frontier models & labs",
      "headline": "OpenAI safety and transparency lead David Robinson resigns days after three researchers were dismissed",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Let's start with OpenAI. Benzinga reported on October 3rd that David Robinson, a leader on OpenAI's Safety Systems team, resigned last week. An OpenAI spokesperson confirmed the departure to Business Insider on Friday." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "What was his job?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Safety and transparency. He helped develop and publish the system cards that describe OpenAI's models, and before that he led the company's policy planning." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "This is an update on yesterday's episode, when OpenAI said it had parted ways with three researchers over how they handled sensitive company information. Robinson's exit also follows that of the safety head, Johannes Heidecke, earlier this year." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "One caveat, and it's a real one. The original reporting here is Business Insider's, and that page wouldn't open for either of our fetchers, so everything we have comes from a single source, Benzinga's account of it. Robinson didn't comment, and OpenAI didn't respond to Benzinga." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "On to the research, where the most striking result today is about scaffolding rather than about a new model." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "MIT visual harness lifts Claude Opus 5.0 from 40.68 to 100.00 on the ARC-AGI-3 efficiency score",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "A preprint on arXiv describes a visual harness called VISTA. The paper says it improves Claude Opus 5.0's Relative Human Action Efficiency score on ARC-AGI-3 from 40.68 to a perfect 100.00, with the model completing all 25 public games using 57.4% fewer actions than first-time human participants." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "What does the harness actually do?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "It gives a general-purpose multimodal model what the authors call long-horizon vision. It keeps a lossless visual memory of past observations in their original form, and the model can retrieve those observations and reorganise its visual input as it reasons." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "And the point is that the model underneath didn't change. The organisers' own baseline, with no programs, puts that same Claude Opus 5.0 at 40.68." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Worth saying this is a preprint, so it isn't peer reviewed, and the numbers are the authors' own. The arXiv HTML lists the affiliation of its five authors as the Massachusetts Institute of Technology." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Argo-Bench: Claude Opus 5.5, strongest of 14 models, scores 95 or higher on 34.8% of 210 enterprise data tasks",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "Also on arXiv, a benchmark called Argo-Bench, from a company called TextQL. It puts 14 frontier and open-weight models on enterprise data work." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "How did they do?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The paper says the strongest, Claude Opus 5.5, solves 34.8% of tasks and averages 59.5 points. Nine of the fourteen average below 35. The authors write that models often analyse the wrong quantity or optimise the wrong objective." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And the tasks are unusually large. There are 210 of them, over a simulated New York City food delivery platform with 81 million orders in 2024, exported to a warehouse of 235 tables and 7.5 billion rows." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The grader also scores consequences rather than queries. The agent files an action, like banning a fraudulent account or allocating a courier incentive budget, and the simulator scores what that action did." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "A preprint again, not peer reviewed, and arXiv is the only source we have for it. The benchmark is also built by a company that sells data-agent software, so the incentive runs toward a hard benchmark." }
      ]
    },
    {
